{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"antelopine-powellite-rooster","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# rebar's hardcoded Jira vocabularies disagree with a real DC instance in four places\n\n## Provenance\n\nFound by the capability-map job (run **30863672922**, ticket 259b) on its first execution — Jira DC\n8.17.1, Server + Software licensed, a Scrum-template project. Every item below is a direct\ncomparison of a rebar constant against what the instance actually offers, with the raw REST\nrequest/response recorded in that run's `evidence.json`.\n\nNone of these would surface as a test failure today. Three are silent by construction.\n\n## The four defects\n\n**1. Status `IDEA` does not exist.** `LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA['idea'] = 'IDEA'`\n(`jira_family/value_maps.py`), but no status of that name exists in ANY workflow bound to ANY issue\ntype on the instance. Every SCRUM type uses \"Software Simplified Workflow for Project SCRUM\", whose\nonly statuses are To Do / In Progress / Done. **A local `idea` ticket can therefore never sync its\nstatus** — the mapped value matches nothing. Silent: the transition simply never resolves.\n\n**2. Label limit is 254, not 255.** `JIRA_LABEL_MAX_CHARS = 255` (`value_maps.py:58`) is INCLUSIVE,\nbut the instance REJECTS a 255-character label. The effective ceiling is 254. So a label of exactly\n255 chars is fitted to the limit and then rejected by Jira — and per bug 6afc, ACLI exits 0 on that\nclass of rejection, so it fails silently and re-emits. (`JIRA_SUMMARY_MAX_CHARS = 254` was checked\nin the same pass and IS correct.)\n\n**3. Two distinct issue types are both named `Task`** (ids 10003 and 10004). Any name-based type\nresolution is ambiguous and may bind to the wrong one, non-deterministically. rebar's\n`LOCAL_TYPE_TO_JIRA` maps by NAME.\n\n**4. Three link relations are unrepresentable on stock Jira** — `supersedes`, `discovered_from`,\n`caused_by` have no corresponding link type. `RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK` maps only `blocks`,\n`depends_on` and `relates_to`, so those three silently do not sync. Whether that is intended is not\nrecorded anywhere; it should be a documented decision rather than an accident.\n\n## What the same pass CONFIRMED as correct, so it is not re-litigated\n\nIssue types Bug/Story/Task/Epic all present; all five priorities (Highest..Lowest) present; link\ntypes Blocks and Relates present; **and the link DIRECTIONS verified end-to-end** — `blocks` and\n`depends_on` are true inverses of the single `Blocks` type, `relates_to` symmetric, all three\nconfirmed by creating real links and reading both endpoints. That was the highest-risk vocabulary\n(a direction error inverts a dependency silently) and it is correct.\n\nTransition names also equal their destination status names on this workflow, so bug 7f93's\nname-vs-destination hazard does not currently bite here — but that is a property of THIS workflow,\nnot a guarantee.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] each of the four is triaged: fixed, or recorded as an accepted limitation with a reason\n- [ ] the `idea` status gap has a decision — map it to an existing status, or state that `idea`\n      tickets deliberately do not sync status\n- [ ] the label limit is corrected to the enforced value, with a test that would catch an\n      off-by-one against the instance rather than against a constant\n- [ ] the duplicate `Task` name is handled by resolving types by ID where available, or the\n      ambiguity is documented as harmless with evidence\n- [ ] the three unmapped relations are documented as deliberate, or mapped\n- [ ] the declared environment contract (3fe5) asserts these so a future instance change fails loudly","id":"2e47-ae62-c0cf-48a0","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"rebar's hardcoded Jira vocabularies disagree with a real DC instance in four places"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785803376148609001,"uuid":"5e37f927-7759-4d34-92ca-a8bf6520c1cc"}